{
  "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
  "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Federal Circuit affirmed an award of attorney fees under the Equal Access to Justice Act, or EAJA, to Petitioner Richlin Security Services Company, but it denied Richlin an award for paralegal services at market rates on the ground that statutory attorney fees do not include work done by paralegals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Wolfman, what about the cap that's not present in 1988 and is present in EAJA?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Are there some disutilities from an economic standpoint in having two structures: Jenkins for one kind of cases, EAJA in the other cases as the government wants?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ANTHONY YANG ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Do you know of any other case where we used -- I mean it just gets worse and worse. Do you know any other case where we've used the legislative history of a vetoed bill to determine the meaning of a later bill that was not vetoed?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, the theory of the bill was that attorney's fees would be based at prevailing market rates, and that prevailing market rates would embody a certain set of costs that might be reimbursed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The cap in EAJA was imposed from the very beginning."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I go back to the point, because I think you were cut off before you went into the full legislative history. I'm still concerned about the argument that even if they are not fees but rather costs, that the costs should be those that are billed to the client."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. YANG",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0002-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. YANG: We often don't bill them separately."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, but I think Justice Breyer is correct, isn't he, that there was no reason for the Senate to sort of redo a report that they had already done on a bill that was substantially identical?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Wolfman",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fde073e9851f-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There was an extensive House report accompanying that legislation. There was no Senate report."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1717",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "fde073e9851ff52b11e6a152caa6f43cb4dfa0b79593252e2697764a33dc2467",
